# Description:

## The rationale for doing this is that the migration from ESLINT version 8.x to 9.x:

- Is eventually going to be mandatory.
- It's kind of difficult.
- The automated migration script doesn't work very well, or at all.

## And also our stack for linting and prettifying:

- Has become kind of cumbersome and difficult to understand and maintain.
- Has **way too many** dependencies.
- It's slow.

### Pros of doing this:

- Migration was easy.
- It's fast.
- Removed around 15 separate dependencies from `package.json`. This means less time installing `dAppBooster`, less time updating dependencies, less maintenance, etc.
- It's really fast.
- Removed several separate config files for ESLINT and Prettier.
- Simplified rules and config. Only one file.
- No, really, it's fast.
- I think it's a bit stricter than ESLINT and Prettier.
- It's pretty compatible with both ESLINT and Prettier.

### Cons:

- It was me who had to do it.
- It was me who tried to migrate ESLINT to version 9 and lost quite a few hours trying to do it.
- You need to add yet another plugin to VSCode or [whatever IDE](https://biomejs.dev/guides/editors/first-party-extensions/) you're using if you want it to support Biome: https://biomejs.dev/reference/vscode/ (this might need some extra work on the dev's part, but you have to do it only once).
- Some rules might not exist or work differently than those from ESLINT and Prettier.
- Some of the recommended rules are different than ESLINT's and Prettier's recommended rules.
- It's not so well known as ESLINT / Prettier.
- It's already done, it works.

### Notes:

- I think this is the path we should follow, the other option being to start from zero with ESLINT v9 (remember: migration doesn't work).
- I think this is easier and it's obviously faster.
- I added "ignores" to the rules I don't know how to fix. The app was already working OK those "issues" present, so I don't think they're gonna be a problem.
- Suggest any rules you think may need changing. I think the defaults are pretty OK.

